\", \"expected\": \"Only one MCU should be populated. If the two parts are alternate footprints, one should be marked do-not-populate or kept out of the assembled BOM, since two driven outputs on the same pins would contend.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"6\\\") (pinfunction \\\"PB1\\\") (pintype \\\"bidirectional\\\")) ... (node (ref \\\"U3\\\") (pin \\\"6\\\") (pinfunction \\\"PB1\\\") (pintype \\\"bidirectional\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"8\\\") (pinfunction \\\"VCC\\\") (pintype \\\"power_in\\\")) ... (node (ref \\\"U3\\\") (pin \\\"8\\\") (pinfunction \\\"VCC\\\") (pintype \\\"power_in\\\"))\"}], \"impact\": \"If both devices are soldered, their outputs drive the PWM, RST and TACH lines against each other, causing contention, unreliable control of the fan and possible damage to the pins. This may be a deliberate dual-footprint arrangement, in which case it is only a risk if both are populated.\", \"confidence\": 0.2}], \"summary\": \"The netlist shows the PWM pull-up R5 tied to the unregulated VCC rail with an unusually low 1k value, which is inconsistent with the other 10k pull-ups and the 5 V logic domain. The spec describes an unrelated repository and gives no fan_controller values, so this verdict rests on standard practice; the parallel U2/U3 MCUs are a lower-confidence second candidate.\"}",
